  urldate = {2019-04-13},
  abstract = {This paper shows that the focus of Australia's `declared' defence policy has oscillated between local and regional defence, whereas its `operational' policy---the views contained in internal planning and guidance documents---has taken a mid-course, focusing on defending Australia's northern approaches. Australia's two policy domains coincided briefly in the mid-1980s but have since diverged as we have again begun to emphasise regional defence. This shift could signal the end of `defence self-reliance'. While representing a setback for the Hawke government, such a result is necessary as Australia's `operational' policy is flawed and in need of replacement The danger is that, as in the past, Australian governments and their advisers will continue to adjust their rhetoric rather than their real policies to our changing circumstances.},
