  doi = {10/dq4f3d},
  urldate = {2024-05-16},
  abstract = {Scholars studying military buildups (arms races or unilateral buildups) have focused their attention on the escalation phase of military conflict. This study shifts the focus, analyzing whether military buildups had any role in the onset of that conflict. Three theoretical possibilities are identified and investigated. The findings indicate that buildups were generally unrelated to subsequent conflict involvement. In addition, large arms increases did not make a nation or rivals more likely to initiate acts or threats involving military force. The results remained unchanged despite controls for measurement differences, periodicity, national differences, and possible delayed effects from the buildups. Implications for research and policy are discussed.},
