  abstract = {This article formulates a model of alliance persistence and evolution to account for two aspects of behaviour that are largely neglected by traditional alliance theory. It argues that states that encounter common threats to military and nonmilitary interests (like political and economic stability) are likely to institutionalize their military pacts. This process raises material and normative obstacles to exiting the alliance, even as the performance of the alliance falters or the strategic context undergoes fundamental changes. The article tests several of the model's hypotheses by engaging in a comparative analysis of two Cold War alliance systems: the North Atlantic Treaty Organization and the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization. /// Cet article pr{\'e}sente un mod{\`e}le de persistance et d'{\'e}volution des alliances afin de cerner deux comportements largement n{\'e}glig{\'e}s par les th{\'e}ories traditionnelles des alliances. On y postule que les {\'E}tats faisant face {\`a} des menaces communes {\`a} leurs int{\'e}r{\^e}ts militaires ou non militaires (par exemple la stabilit{\'e} politique et {\'e}conomique) auront tendance {\`a} institutionnaliser leurs pactes militaires. Ce processus cr{\'e}e des barri{\`e}res mat{\'e}rielles et normatives {\`a} la sortie de l'alliance, m{\^e}me lorsque la performance de celle-ci diminue ou que le contexte strat{\'e}gique subit des transformations fondamentales. L'article teste plusieurs hypoth{\`e}ses d{\'e}riv{\'e}es dudit mod{\`e}le en analysant de fa{\c c}on comparative les deux syst{\`e}mes d'alliances de la guerre froide : l'Organisation du trait{\'e} de l'Atlantique Nord (OTAN) et l'Organisation du trait{\'e} de l'Asie du Sud-Est (OTASE).},
